// A function may not return a borrowed reference to one of its own locals.
//
// ref-flow asserts the other half of the same rule, between two variables. This
// half is the function boundary: the caller reads the returned reference after
// the frame that held the borrowed-from value is gone, so what it points at no
// longer exists. coneref/reflifefn.html states the rule -- a borrowed reference
// is restricted "from living beyond the scope they were created in", and a
// violation "result[s] in a compiler error" -- and nothing enforced it here
// until returnFlow in ir/stmt/return.c was written.
//
// Borrow scopes count outward: 0 is a global, 1 is a parameter, 2 or more is a
// local. Only 2 and up escape, which is why the returns of a parameter borrow
// in ref-success and of a parameter slice in collection-success stay legal.
//
// Every diagnostic here is a flow diagnostic, so each function must type check
// cleanly for its own to be reported. They are separate functions rather than
// one, because a function has one return.

fn escapesImplicitly() &i32 {
  mut n = 3
  &n                        //~ ErrorEscape:3 "Returned borrowed reference outlives the local value it points to"
}

fn escapesExplicitly() &i32 {
  mut n = 3
  return &n                 //~ ErrorEscape:10 "Returned borrowed reference outlives the local value it points to"
}

// A slice carries the same scope as the reference it is built from, so the same
// rule reaches it. collection-flow-escape asserts this between two variables.
fn escapesAsSlice() &[]i32 {
  mut b [3; i32] = [1, 2, 3]
  &[]b                      //~ ErrorEscape:3 "Returned borrowed reference outlives the local value it points to"
}

// What the check does not reach, established here rather than claimed: a borrow
// laundered through a variable. The scope of a borrow lives on the type node the
// borrow expression itself produced, and an assignment does not carry it onto
// the variable's declared type, so by the time this returns 'chosen' the return
// sees a plain '&i32' of global scope. assignlvalrtype does not catch it either,
// because 'chosen' and 'outer' share a scope and storing one in the other is
// legal. Closing this needs lifetimes tracked through variables, which is what
// coneref/reflifefn.html's unimplemented annotations are for.
//
// This function compiles clean, and that is the assertion.
fn escapesThroughAVariable() &i32 {
  mut chosen &i32
  imm outer = 5
  chosen = &outer
  chosen
}
